  "content": "\\section{Methodology}\n\nThis section presents our Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol with Real-Time Traffic Information for Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) networks. We first introduce the overall system architecture, followed by detailed descriptions of each component, the routing algorithms being compared, the carbon emission calculation methodology, and the simulation environment.\n\n\\subsection{Two-Layer Architecture}\n\nOur proposed framework employs a two-layer architecture that seamlessly integrates real-time traffic information with carbon emission metrics to enable adaptive routing decisions in CPI networks, as illustrated in Figure~\\ref{fig:framework}.\n\n\\begin{figure}[ht]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{framework_diagram.png}\n    \\caption{Two-layer architecture of the Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol integrating real-time traffic information with carbon emission metrics. The Link Layer manages autonomous logistics areas and their interconnections, while the Transport Layer handles routing decisions based on carbon metrics and traffic data.}\n    \\label{fig:framework}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsubsection{Link Layer}\nThe Link Layer is responsible for managing the organization of autonomous logistics areas and their interconnections. It consists of the following components:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Traffic Data Collection}: Continuously gathers real-time traffic information from various sources, including road sensors, GPS data, and historical traffic patterns.\n    \\item \\textbf{Network Topology Management}: Maintains an up-to-date representation of the transportation network, including nodes (logistics centers, warehouses, distribution centers) and links (roads, highways, connections).\n    \\item \\textbf{Traffic Condition Database}: Stores and processes current and historical traffic conditions, enabling the system to predict congestion patterns.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{Transport Layer}\nThe Transport Layer handles routing decisions based on carbon metrics and real-time traffic information. Key components include:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Carbon Emission Calculator}: Computes carbon emissions for different routes based on vehicle type, load, distance, and current speed derived from traffic conditions.\n    \\item \\textbf{Dynamic Route Recalculation}: Continuously evaluates routing decisions when significant traffic changes are detected.\n    \\item \\textbf{Multi-criteria Decision Making}: Balances carbon emissions reduction with delivery time constraints using a weighted objective function.\n    \\item \\textbf{Traffic Prediction Model}: Anticipates future congestion and proactively adjusts routes to minimize both emissions and delays.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Carbon-Aware Routing Algorithms}\n\nWe implemented and compared four different routing algorithms, each with varying degrees of carbon awareness and traffic adaptability.\n\n\\subsubsection{Baseline Shortest Path Algorithm}\nThe Baseline Shortest Path algorithm represents traditional routing approaches that optimize for distance or time without considering carbon emissions or real-time traffic updates. This algorithm uses the standard Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm with static travel times.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{Baseline Shortest Path Algorithm}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\Function{BaselineShortestPath}{$origin, destination, network$}\n    \\State $graph \\gets network.graph$\n    \\State $path \\gets$ ShortestPath($graph, origin, destination, weight=\\text{'base\\_travel\\_time'}$)\n    \\State $total\\_distance, total\\_time \\gets 0, 0$\n    \\For{$i = 0$ \\textbf{to} $|path| - 2$}\n        \\State $from\\_node \\gets path[i]$\n        \\State $to\\_node \\gets path[i+1]$\n        \\State $segment\\_distance \\gets graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].distance$\n        \\State $segment\\_time \\gets graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].base\\_travel\\_time$\n        \\State $total\\_distance \\gets total\\_distance + segment\\_distance$\n        \\State $total\\_time \\gets total\\_time + segment\\_time$\n    \\EndFor\n    \\State \\Return $path, total\\_distance, total\\_time$\n\\EndFunction\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\n\\subsubsection{Static Carbon-Aware Algorithm}\nThe Static Carbon-Aware algorithm optimizes for carbon emissions but does not account for real-time traffic conditions. It creates a carbon-weighted graph where edge weights represent the estimated carbon emissions for traversing that segment.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{Static Carbon-Aware Algorithm}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\Function{StaticCarbonAware}{$origin, destination, network, vehicle\\_info$}\n    \\State $graph \\gets$ Copy($network.graph$)\n    \\For{each $edge (from\\_node, to\\_node)$ in $graph$}\n        \\State $distance \\gets graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].distance$\n        \\State $base\\_time \\gets graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].base\\_travel\\_time$\n        \\State $speed \\gets (distance / base\\_time) \\times 60$ \\Comment{Convert minutes to hours}\n        \\State $emissions \\gets$ CalculateSegmentEmissions($from\\_node, to\\_node, distance, vehicle\\_info, speed$)\n        \\State $graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].carbon\\_weight \\gets emissions$\n    \\EndFor\n    \\State $path \\gets$ ShortestPath($graph, origin, destination, weight=\\text{'carbon\\_weight'}$)\n    \\State Calculate $total\\_distance, total\\_time, total\\_emissions$ for $path$\n    \\State \\Return $path, total\\_distance, total\\_time, total\\_emissions$\n\\EndFunction\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\n\\subsubsection{Traffic-Adaptive Algorithm}\nThe Traffic-Adaptive algorithm considers real-time traffic conditions to optimize travel time but does not consider carbon emissions. This represents traditional navigation systems that adjust routes based on congestion levels.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{Traffic-Adaptive Algorithm}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\Function{TrafficAdaptive}{$origin, destination, network, current\\_time$}\n    \\State $network$.UpdateTrafficConditions($current\\_time$)\n    \\State $graph \\gets network.graph$ \\Comment{Graph now contains current traffic information}\n    \\State $path \\gets$ ShortestPath($graph, origin, destination, weight=\\text{'current\\_travel\\_time'}$)\n    \\State $total\\_distance, total\\_time \\gets 0, 0$\n    \\For{$i = 0$ \\textbf{to} $|path| - 2$}\n        \\State $from\\_node \\gets path[i]$\n        \\State $to\\_node \\gets path[i+1]$\n        \\State $segment\\_distance \\gets graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].distance$\n        \\State $segment\\_time \\gets network$.GetTravelTime($from\\_node, to\\_node$) \\Comment{Current traffic-aware time}\n        \\State $total\\_distance \\gets total\\_distance + segment\\_distance$\n        \\State $total\\_time \\gets total\\_time + segment\\_time$\n    \\EndFor\n    \\State \\Return $path, total\\_distance, total\\_time$\n\\EndFunction\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\n\\subsubsection{Carbon-Aware Adaptive Algorithm (Proposed Method)}\nOur proposed Carbon-Aware Adaptive algorithm integrates both carbon emission optimization and real-time traffic adaptation. It uses a multi-objective approach that balances carbon emissions and travel time through a weighted objective function.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}\n\\caption{Carbon-Aware Adaptive Algorithm (Proposed Method)}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\Function{CarbonAwareAdaptive}{$origin, destination, network, vehicle\\_info, current\\_time, carbon\\_weight=0.5$}\n    \\State $network$.UpdateTrafficConditions($current\\_time$)\n    \\State $graph \\gets$ Copy($network.graph$)\n    \\State $all\\_times \\gets [], all\\_emissions \\gets []$ \\Comment{For normalization}\n    \n    \\For{each $edge (from\\_node, to\\_node)$ in $graph$}\n        \\State $current\\_time \\gets network$.GetTravelTime($from\\_node, to\\_node$)\n        \\State $all\\_times$.Append($current\\_time$)\n        \n        \\State $distance \\gets graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].distance$\n        \\State $speed \\gets (distance / current\\_time) \\times 60$ \\Comment{Convert to km/h}\n        \\State $emissions \\gets$ CalculateSegmentEmissions($from\\_node, to\\_node, distance, vehicle\\_info, speed$)\n        \\State $all\\_emissions$.Append($emissions$)\n    \\EndFor\n    \n    \\State $max\\_time \\gets$ Max($all\\_times$)\n    \\State $max\\_emissions \\gets$ Max($all\\_emissions$)\n    \n    \\For{each $edge (from\\_node, to\\_node)$ in $graph$}\n        \\State $current\\_time \\gets network$.GetTravelTime($from\\_node, to\\_node$)\n        \\State $distance \\gets graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].distance$\n        \\State $speed \\gets (distance / current\\_time) \\times 60$\n        \\State $emissions \\gets$ CalculateSegmentEmissions($from\\_node, to\\_node, distance, vehicle\\_info, speed$)\n        \n        \\State $normalized\\_time \\gets current\\_time / max\\_time$\n        \\State $normalized\\_emissions \\gets emissions / max\\_emissions$\n        \\State $combined\\_weight \\gets (1 - carbon\\_weight) \\times normalized\\_time + carbon\\_weight \\times normalized\\_emissions$\n        \\State $graph[from\\_node][to\\_node].multi\\_weight \\gets combined\\_weight$\n    \\EndFor\n    \n    \\State $path \\gets$ ShortestPath($graph, origin, destination, weight=\\text{'multi\\_weight'}$)\n    \\State Calculate $total\\_distance, total\\_time, total\\_emissions$ for $path$\n    \\State \\Return $path, total\\_distance, total\\_time, total\\_emissions$\n\\EndFunction\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\n\\subsection{Carbon Emission Calculation Methodology}\n\nOur carbon emission calculation methodology accounts for various factors that affect transportation-related emissions, providing a comprehensive assessment for route optimization.\n\n\\subsubsection{Emission Factor Model}\n\nThe emission calculation is based on the following factors:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Vehicle Type}: Different vehicle types have distinct base emission factors. We support six types:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Light Truck: 0.25 kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km (empty)\n        \\item Medium Truck: 0.45 kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km (empty)\n        \\item Heavy Truck: 0.75 kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km (empty)\n        \\item Van: 0.18 kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km (empty)\n        \\item Electric Van: 0.05 kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km (from electricity generation)\n        \\item Electric Truck: 0.08 kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km (from electricity generation)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Load Factor}: Additional emissions based on cargo load:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Light Truck: 0.15 additional kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km per ton\n        \\item Medium Truck: 0.12 additional kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km per ton\n        \\item Heavy Truck: 0.08 additional kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km per ton\n        \\item Van: 0.20 additional kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km per ton\n        \\item Electric Van: 0.03 additional kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km per ton\n        \\item Electric Truck: 0.04 additional kg CO\\textsubscript{2}/km per ton\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Speed Efficiency}: Based on empirical data showing that vehicles are most efficient at moderate speeds (typically 50-60 km/h), with efficiency decreasing at both lower and higher speeds. We model this using a speed efficiency curve for each vehicle type.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Distance}: The total distance traveled, which directly affects total emissions.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{Emission Calculation Formula}\n\nFor a single route segment, carbon emissions are calculated as:\n\n\\begin{equation}\nEmissions = (Base_{emissions} + Load_{emissions}) \\times Speed_{multiplier}\n\\end{equation}\n\nWhere:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $Base_{emissions} = BaseEmissionFactor \\times Distance$\n    \\item $Load_{emissions} = LoadEmissionFactor \\times Load \\times Distance$\n    \\item $Speed_{multiplier}$ is derived from the speed efficiency curve for the vehicle type\n\\end{itemize}\n\nFor a complete route, total emissions are the sum of emissions for all segments:\n\n\\begin{equation}\nTotalEmissions = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} Emissions(segment_i)\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Environment and Network Model}\n\n\\subsubsection{Network Model}\n\nOur simulation environment is based on a representation of the Greater Bay Area transportation network in China, which includes major cities such as Hong Kong, Shenzhen, Guangzhou, Macau, Zhuhai, Dongguan, Foshan, and Zhongshan. The network model consists of:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Nodes}: Represent cities, warehouses, distribution centers, and logistics hubs, each with geographical coordinates and node types.\n    \\item \\textbf{Links}: Represent transportation connections between nodes, characterized by:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Distance (kilometers)\n        \\item Base travel time (minutes under normal conditions)\n        \\item Capacity (vehicles per hour)\n        \\item Road type (highway, arterial, local)\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{Traffic Simulation}\n\nThe traffic simulation component models dynamic traffic conditions throughout a 24-hour cycle, with the following features:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Time-based Traffic Patterns}: Traffic conditions vary by hour, with defined patterns for night (low traffic), morning rush (high), midday (moderate), evening rush (peak), and evening (decreasing).\n    \\item \\textbf{Congestion Multipliers}: Each hour has an associated traffic multiplier that adjusts travel times:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Night (0-5): 0.2-0.5x multiplier (low traffic)\n        \\item Morning Rush (6-9): 0.8-1.6x multiplier (high traffic)\n        \\item Midday (10-14): 0.9-1.2x multiplier (moderate traffic)\n        \\item Evening Rush (15-19): 1.2-1.9x multiplier (peak traffic)\n        \\item Evening (20-23): 0.5-1.0x multiplier (decreasing traffic)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Random Variation}: A random variation factor (±20\\%) is applied to traffic conditions to simulate the inherent unpredictability of real-world traffic.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Experimental Scenarios}\n\nWe designed a comprehensive set of experimental scenarios to evaluate the performance of our proposed Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol. These scenarios are designed to test the algorithms under various conditions:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Traffic Congestion Levels}:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Low traffic conditions (off-peak hours)\n        \\item Medium traffic conditions (regular daytime hours)\n        \\item High traffic conditions (peak rush hours)\n        \\item Mixed traffic conditions (varying throughout the day)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Vehicle Types and Carbon Emission Factors}:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Light vehicles (vans, light trucks)\n        \\item Medium trucks\n        \\item Heavy trucks\n        \\item Electric vehicles (vans and trucks)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Order Density}:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Low density (sparse orders, typical of off-peak times)\n        \\item Medium density (regular business hours)\n        \\item High density (peak e-commerce periods)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Time Constraints}:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Urgent delivery (time-critical shipments)\n        \\item Regular delivery (standard service levels)\n        \\item Economy delivery (carbon-optimized, relaxed time constraints)\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n\nTo comprehensively assess the performance of the different routing algorithms, we use the following metrics:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Effectiveness Metrics}:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Total carbon emissions (kg CO\\textsubscript{2})\n        \\item Carbon emissions reduction percentage (compared to baseline)\n        \\item Average delivery time (minutes)\n        \\item Delivery time increase percentage (compared to time-optimized routing)\n        \\item Carbon efficiency (emissions per ton-km)\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Efficiency Metrics}:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Computational time for route calculation (seconds)\n        \\item Number of routing table updates\n        \\item Memory usage for routing tables\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Adaptability Metrics}:\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Response time to traffic changes (seconds)\n        \\item Route stability under different traffic patterns (number of route changes)\n        \\item Performance degradation in extreme congestion (percentage)\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Implementation Framework}\n\nOur implementation consists of three main software modules:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Network Simulator (network\\_simulator.py)}: Creates and manages the transportation network, handles traffic condition updates, and provides travel time calculations.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Carbon Calculator (carbon\\_calculator.py)}: Calculates carbon emissions for route segments based on vehicle type, load, speed, and distance.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Routing Algorithms (routing\\_algorithms.py)}: Implements all four routing algorithms and provides functionality for comparing their performance.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe entire system is implemented in Python, using NetworkX for graph operations and NumPy for numerical calculations. The implementation follows a modular design approach that allows for easy extension and modification of individual components.",
