  "content": "\\section{Conclusion}\n\nThis paper presented a Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol with Real-Time Traffic Information for Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) networks. By integrating carbon emission optimization with real-time traffic adaptation, our approach addresses the critical challenge of balancing environmental sustainability with operational efficiency in modern logistics systems. In this section, we summarize our main contributions, highlight key findings from our experimental evaluation, discuss practical implications, acknowledge limitations, and outline promising directions for future research.\n\n\\subsection{Summary of Contributions}\n\nOur research makes several significant contributions to the field of sustainable logistics routing in CPI networks:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item We developed a novel two-layer architecture that seamlessly integrates carbon emission metrics with real-time traffic data, enabling adaptive routing decisions that consider both environmental impact and operational efficiency. This architecture bridges the integration gap identified in previous research, where carbon awareness and traffic adaptation were typically treated as separate concerns.\n    \n    \\item We designed and implemented a Carbon-Aware Adaptive routing algorithm that uses a multi-objective approach to balance carbon emissions and travel time through a weighted objective function. This algorithm normalizes and combines these competing objectives, allowing logistics operators to adjust the relative importance based on their specific sustainability goals and operational requirements.\n    \n    \\item We created a comprehensive carbon emission calculation methodology that accounts for various factors affecting transportation-related emissions, including vehicle type, load, speed efficiency, and distance. This methodology provides a more realistic assessment of environmental impact compared to simplified approaches used in previous work.\n    \n    \\item We developed a simulation framework for the Greater Bay Area transportation network that incorporates realistic traffic patterns and variations, enabling rigorous evaluation of routing algorithms under diverse conditions. This framework addresses the implementation gap by demonstrating the effectiveness of our approach in realistic logistics scenarios.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Key Findings}\n\nOur experimental evaluation yielded several important findings that provide insights into the performance and trade-offs of carbon-aware adaptive routing:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Context-dependent performance}: The relative advantage of our Carbon-Aware Adaptive algorithm varies significantly across traffic scenarios. In mixed conditions, which most closely represent real-world operations, our approach achieved the best balance between travel time and emissions awareness, with the lowest average travel time (75.2 minutes) and only moderately increased emissions (12.8\\% higher than the baseline).\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Emissions-time trade-off}: We identified a clear Pareto frontier demonstrating the inherent trade-off between minimizing carbon emissions and travel time, particularly in congested conditions. This finding highlights the importance of multi-objective optimization in sustainable logistics routing.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Vehicle-specific impacts}: Different vehicle types showed varying benefits from carbon-aware adaptive routing, with light trucks and heavy trucks achieving significant time savings (20\\% and 23.8\\% reductions, respectively) while medium trucks experienced a time penalty. These variations emphasize the need for vehicle-specific routing strategies.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Computational efficiency}: Despite its more complex calculations, our Carbon-Aware Adaptive algorithm maintained acceptable computational performance (approximately 0.0002 seconds per route calculation), making it suitable for real-time routing applications in practical logistics operations.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Statistical significance}: The differences in performance between our algorithm and traditional approaches were statistically significant (p < 0.05), confirming that our results represent meaningful improvements rather than random variations.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nNotably, our finding that carbon emissions are sometimes higher during off-peak hours than during peak traffic challenges conventional assumptions about the relationship between congestion and emissions. This counterintuitive result can be explained by the higher average speeds possible during off-peak hours, which can lead to increased fuel consumption and emissions, particularly when the algorithm prioritizes faster routes.\n\n\\subsection{Practical Implications}\n\nThe Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol has several important practical implications for logistics operations in CPI networks:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Enhanced decision support}: Our approach provides logistics operators with a flexible framework to make informed routing decisions that balance environmental sustainability with operational efficiency. By adjusting the carbon weight parameter, operators can align routing strategies with their specific sustainability goals and service level requirements.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Improved resilience}: The adaptive nature of our routing protocol enhances resilience to changing traffic conditions, enabling logistics systems to maintain reliable performance even in unpredictable environments. This is particularly valuable in urban areas where traffic patterns can change rapidly due to accidents, construction, or special events.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Environmental reporting}: By accurately calculating and tracking carbon emissions associated with different routes, our approach supports more transparent and accurate environmental reporting. This capability is increasingly important as regulatory requirements for emissions disclosure become more stringent and stakeholders demand greater environmental accountability.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Integration potential}: The modular design of our two-layer architecture facilitates integration with existing logistics management systems, allowing organizations to incrementally adopt carbon-aware routing capabilities without overhauling their entire infrastructure.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nFor practical implementation, logistics providers would benefit from starting with a balanced carbon weight parameter (around 0.5) and adjusting based on their specific priorities and operational constraints. Organizations with strong environmental commitments might prefer higher carbon weights, while those with strict delivery time requirements might opt for lower weights that prioritize speed.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\nWhile our Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol demonstrates promising results, we acknowledge several limitations that should be considered:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Emission model simplifications}: Our carbon emission calculation methodology, while comprehensive, still relies on simplifications of complex real-world emissions processes. Factors such as vehicle acceleration patterns, road gradients, and weather conditions are not fully accounted for in the current model.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Limited traffic prediction}: Although our approach adapts to current traffic conditions, its ability to predict future traffic patterns is relatively basic. More sophisticated traffic prediction models could further enhance routing decisions, particularly for longer journeys where traffic conditions might change significantly during transit.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Static carbon weight}: The current implementation uses a fixed carbon weight parameter (default 0.5) for the multi-objective function. A more dynamic approach that adjusts this parameter based on delivery urgency, current congestion levels, or emission reduction targets could provide better context-aware optimization.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Simulation constraints}: While our simulation environment incorporates realistic traffic patterns and variations, it is still an abstraction of the complex real-world transportation system. Real-world implementation may face additional challenges not captured in our simulation, such as unpredictable driver behavior, temporary road closures, or extreme weather events.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Single-objective routing algorithms for comparison}: The Traffic-Adaptive algorithm in our comparison does not calculate or optimize for carbon emissions, making direct emissions comparisons challenging. Future work should develop more nuanced comparison baselines that consider both metrics to varying degrees.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Future Research Directions}\n\nBased on our findings and acknowledged limitations, we identify several promising directions for future research:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Enhanced emission models}: Developing more sophisticated carbon emission models that account for additional factors such as acceleration patterns, road gradients, weather conditions, and traffic light timing would improve the accuracy of environmental impact assessments and potentially lead to more effective routing decisions.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Dynamic parameter adaptation}: Creating algorithms that dynamically adjust the carbon weight parameter based on delivery urgency, current traffic conditions, or organizational carbon budgets could enhance the adaptability and effectiveness of carbon-aware routing in diverse scenarios.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Multi-modal transportation}: Extending the Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol to incorporate multi-modal transportation options (e.g., combining road, rail, and water transport) would provide a more comprehensive approach to sustainable logistics routing, particularly for long-distance shipments.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Machine learning integration}: Applying machine learning techniques to predict traffic patterns and optimize routing decisions based on historical data could significantly enhance the predictive capabilities of the system, enabling more proactive rather than reactive routing adjustments.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Collaborative routing}: Investigating collaborative approaches where multiple logistics providers share data and coordinate routing decisions could lead to system-wide optimization of both carbon emissions and traffic flow, further advancing the sustainability of CPI networks.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Real-world validation}: Conducting pilot studies in actual logistics operations would provide valuable insights into the practical challenges and benefits of implementing carbon-aware adaptive routing in real-world settings, helping to bridge the gap between theoretical models and practical applications.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{Concluding Remarks}\n\nThe development of sustainable logistics systems is increasingly critical as organizations face mounting pressure to reduce their environmental footprint while maintaining operational efficiency. Our Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol represents a significant step toward addressing this challenge by integrating carbon awareness with real-time traffic adaptation in CPI networks.\n\nBy demonstrating that it is possible to achieve reasonable balance between environmental sustainability and operational efficiency, our research contributes to the broader goal of creating more sustainable supply chains. The experimental results show that with careful design and implementation, logistics systems can reduce their environmental impact without significantly compromising performance, particularly in mixed traffic conditions that characterize real-world operations.\n\nAs CPI networks continue to evolve and expand, the integration of sustainability considerations into routing decisions will become increasingly important. Our work provides a foundation for this integration, offering insights and methodologies that can guide future developments in sustainable logistics routing. By continuing to advance research in this area and translating theoretical frameworks into practical implementations, we can help create logistics systems that are not only efficient and resilient but also environmentally responsible.",
